 "desc_header": "IN THIS LESSON",
 "lyrics": "The giraffe is the tallest living land animal, 14 to 19 feet tall; a newborn is about 6 feet tall (National Geographic Kids, 'Giraffe').\nTo drink water, a giraffe must spread its long legs outward and lower its head (National Geographic Kids, 'Giraffe').\nThe African elephant is the largest living land animal; a male can weigh up to 7.5 tons and a newborn 110 to 264 pounds (San Diego Zoo, 'Elephant').\nElephant ears work like a radiator: flapping them on a hot day cools the blood flowing through them (San Diego Zoo, 'Elephant').\nA lion's roar can be heard up to 5 miles (8 km) away (National Geographic); San Diego Zoo's fact sheet gives 5 km (3 miles) under certain conditions. Lions roar to advertise their territory, more often at night and dawn; cubs have spots that fade with age.\nMany male lions have a mane; lions live in prides and may rest up to 20-21 hours a day (San Diego Zoo Wildlife Alliance Library fact sheet, 'Lion').",
 "script": "Hello, friends! I am Milo the bluebird.\nToday is Animal Champions day!\nWe will find the tallest, the biggest, and the loudest animals.\nThree champions are here. Count them with me. One, two, three!\nAre you ready to meet the champions? Let's go!\nWho is the tallest animal in the whole world?\nIs it the elephant? Or is it the giraffe?\nIt is the giraffe!\nHere is the fun fact. The giraffe is the tallest animal on land.\nTall, tall, tall! Taller than a horse. Taller than an elephant. Taller than every animal on land.\nHow tall? Look! A giraffe can peek into an upstairs window.\nA giraffe is as tall as three grown-ups. One, two, three!\nIts long neck helps it reach the leaves at the very top of the tree.\nEven a baby giraffe is tall. On the day it is born, it is as tall as a grown-up!\nBut how does such a tall animal drink water? Hmm.\nIt spreads its long legs wide, and bends its head way, way down. Slurp!\nSay it with me. The giraffe is the tallest!\nCan you stretch up tall like a giraffe? Reach, reach, reach!\nNow bend down low to drink, like a giraffe. Down, down, down. Slurp!\nQuick check! Can a giraffe peek into an upstairs window? Yes, or no?\nYes! Because the giraffe is the tallest of all!\nGive yourself a big clap!\nWho is the biggest animal that walks on land?\nIs it the elephant? Or is it the hippo?\nIt is the elephant!\nHere is the fun fact. The elephant is the biggest animal on land.\nHow heavy is an elephant? As heavy as four cars! Count them with me.\nOne, two, three, four!\nBig body, big ears, and a big, long trunk!\nThe trunk works like a hand and like a hose. It can pick up a tiny peanut, or spray a big shower!\nAnd why does an elephant have such big ears?\nThe big ears work like fans! Flap, flap, flap. They help the elephant cool down.\nA baby elephant is called a calf.\nEven a baby elephant is heavy. It weighs as much as a big grown-up on the day it is born.\nSay it with me. The elephant is the biggest!\nCan you stomp like a big elephant? Stomp, stomp, stomp!\nCan you flap your arms like big elephant ears? Flap, flap, flap!\nSo remember. Big ears are for keeping cool!\nQuick check! An elephant is as heavy as how many cars? Two? Or four?\nFour! One, two, three, four!\nWho has the loudest roar of all the big cats?\nIs it the lion? Or is it the tiger?\nIt is the lion!\nHere is the fun fact. You can hear a lion's roar from up to five miles away!\nFive miles is a long, long way. Like the other side of town!\nWhy does a lion roar? What do you think?\nTo tell everyone, this is my home!\nLook at that big, fluffy mane! Daddy lions grow a mane around their heads.\nLions live in a big family. A lion family is called a pride.\nBaby lions are called cubs. Lion cubs are born with spots!\nAnd lions are sleepy cats. They rest for most of the day!\nSo when do lions roar the most? In the day? Or at night?\nAt night! When the sun goes down, lions get busy, and they roar the most.\nSay it with me. The lion has the loudest roar!\nCan you roar like a lion? One, two, three. Roar!\nQuick check! From how far away can you hear a lion roar? One mile? Or five miles?\nFive miles! What a mighty roar!\nGive yourself a big cheer!\nWow! We met three animal champions. Now it is quiz time!\nGet ready. Think hard!\nWho can peek into an upstairs window?\nThe giraffe! Yes!\nWho is as heavy as four cars?\nThe elephant! That's right!\nWhose roar can you hear from five miles away?\nThe lion! You remembered!\nYou learned so many new things today.\nStretch like a giraffe. Stomp like an elephant. And roar like a lion!\nWhich champion is your favorite?\nThank you for learning with me. Bye-bye, friends! See you next time!",
